00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:08.620
It provides treatment ranking algorithms (open source, anyone can verify).

208
00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:13.660
It provides trial matching services (connects patients to ANY platform).

209
00:22:14.420 --> 00:22:19.140
Existing platforms provide: They provide patient-facing apps and interfaces.

210
00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:23.600
They provide trial management tools. They provide sponsor relationships.

211
00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:28.460
They provide regional expertise. Federated, Not Centralized.

212
00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:32.620
Data doesn't move to a central database. It stays where it is.

213
00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:38.550
Everyone keeps their toys. Epic systems keep their data. Apple Health keeps its data.

214
00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:45.620
Cerner keeps its data. Your framework's protocol just lets you run queries ACROSS systems without moving data.

215
00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:50.340
Like asking every library in the world a question without stealing their books.

216
00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:56.240
Federated data networks already do this with three hundred million plus patient records.

217
00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:05.480
This solves: It solves General Data Protection Regulation and Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, or G.D.P.R.

218
00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:12.500
and H.I.P.A.A. compliance (data never leaves source). It solves privacy concerns (no central honeypot to hack).

219
00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:20.040
It solves vendor cooperation (they keep control). It solves patient trust (data doesn't go to "the cloud").

220
00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:24.300
Why This Works: The Mathematical Impossibility of Committees.

221
00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:31.440
Approximately two hundred F.D.A. bureaucrats decide what eight billion people can try when dying.

222
00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:37.940
Here's the math on why that can't work: The F.D.A. approves approximately fifty new drugs per year.

223
00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:45.160
There are ten thousand known diseases. At that rate, covering every disease once takes two hundred years.

224
00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:52.060
And that's before you account for the fact that different patients respond differently to the same drug based on genetics,

225
00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:59.380
age, comorbidities, and whether they had breakfast. The real bottleneck isn't speed; it's information.

226
00:24:00.200 --> 00:24:06.980
Centralized approval assumes a two hundred-person committee can evaluate what works for eight billion genetically unique

227
00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:16.280
humans. They can't. A decentralized system where millions of patients generate real-world evidence simultaneously processes

228
00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:21.200
more therapeutic information in a month than the F.D.A. generates in a decade.

229
00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:26.640
The F.D.A. doesn't know: What disease YOU have (they haven't met you).

230
00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:30.360
How YOUR body responds to treatments (genetics are unique).

231
00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:36.000
What risks YOU'RE willing to take (some prefer death to side effects, others the reverse).

232
00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:41.080
Whether YOU'D rather die trying or die waiting (only you can answer this).

233
00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:47.700
But they decide for you anyway. This isn't ideology. It's information theory.

234
00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:53.600
You mathematically cannot centralize medical decisions for eight billion unique people.

235
00:24:54.200 --> 00:25:01.080
The Future: Where Death Becomes Embarrassing. Twenty twenty-seven: The Beginning of the End of Dying Slowly.

236
00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:06.360
F.D.A. dot gov becomes actually useful. Millions join trials from home.

237
00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:11.760
The first "Wikipedia disease" gets cured entirely through crowdsourced trials.

